## Authentication endpoints

| Method | Path | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `POST` | `/auth/register` | Create an account when the email is available; the `202` response is identical for new and existing emails. |
| `POST` | `/auth/login` | Accept form fields `username` and `password` and return a bearer token. |
| `POST` | `/auth/logout` | Invalidate every access token for the account and return `204`. |
| `GET` | `/auth/me` | Return the authenticated user or `401`. |

The application reads the JWT signing key from `SECRET_KEY`. The value in
`.env.example` is only a placeholder; replace it with your own secret before
deploying. When `ENVIRONMENT=production`, startup rejects placeholder values and
secrets shorter than 32 characters. `ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES` controls the
token lifetime and defaults to `5` minutes. Each token also carries a unique
`jti` claim; logout and password reset invalidate every token through the
account version.

Registration returns the same message for new and existing emails. Access tokens
are bound to the account token version, so a password reset invalidates older
access tokens. `POST /auth/logout` increments that version and invalidates every
access token for the account.

In production, `EMAIL_BACKEND=console` is rejected so reset and verification
tokens are not written to logs. Set `EMAIL_BACKEND=smtp` and configure SMTP.

Responses include `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `X-Frame-Options: DENY`,
and `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`; HTTPS responses also include HSTS. Proxy
headers are rejected by default. If a reverse proxy fronts the app, set
`FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS` and `uvicorn --forwarded-allow-ips` to only the proxy IPs;
an empty value disables `X-Forwarded-*` trust.

Register a user, request a token, and call the protected route:

```sh
curl -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:8000/auth/register" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email": "user@example.com", "password": "<password>"}'

curl -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:8000/auth/login" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" \
  -d "username=user@example.com&password=<password>"

curl "http://127.0.0.1:8000/auth/me" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer <access_token>"
```
{% endif %}
